==Venue==
[[File:AEK fans during AEK-Marseille game.jpg|thumb|275px|The [[Agia Sophia Stadium]] in [[Athens]] hosted the final.]]
The [[Agia Sophia Stadium]] in [[Athens]], known as the OPAP Arena for sponsorship reasons, is the home of [[AEK Athens F.C.|AEK Athens]], one of Olympiacos' local rivals. The stadium opened in September 2022,<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.ethnos.gr/sports/article/226435/haekepestrepsespitithslamperaegkainiasthnypersygxronhopaparenaolaosaeginansthgiorththsenoshs |title=Η ΑΕΚ επέστρεψε σπίτι της! Λαμπερά εγκαινία στην υπερσύγχρονη OPAP Arena - Όλα όσα έγιναν στη γιορτή της Ένωσης |trans-title=AEK returned home! Sparkling opening at the state-of-the-art OPAP Arena – All that happened at the Union's celebration |work=[[Ethnos (newspaper)|Ethnos]] |date=30 September 2022 |access-date=30 September 2022 |language=el}}</ref> and had a capacity of 31,100.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.aekfc.gr/stp/opap-arena-42938.htm?lang=en |title=OPAP Arena |publisher=AEK Athens F.C. |access-date=29 June 2023}}</ref> The match was the eighth single-leg UEFA club final to be played in Greece, having hosted three European Cup/Champions League finals (in [[1983 European Cup final|1983]], [[1994 UEFA Champions League final|1994]] and [[2007 UEFA Champions League final|2007]]), three [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup|Cup Winners' Cup]] finals (in [[1971 European Cup Winners' Cup final|1971]], [[1973 European Cup Winners' Cup final|1973]] and [[1987 European Cup Winners' Cup final|1987]]) and the [[2023 UEFA Super Cup]].

===Host selection===
On 21 June 2022, UEFA opened the bidding process for the final, which was held in parallel with that of the [[2025 UEFA Conference League final|2025 final]]. Interested bidders could bid for either one or both of the finals. The proposed venues had to include natural grass and be ranked as a [[UEFA stadium categories|UEFA category four stadium]], with a gross capacity of between 30,000 and 50,000 preferred. The bidding timeline was as follows:<ref name="invitation to bid">{{cite magazine |url=https://editorial.uefa.com/resources/0276-157279a17ca3-262ebb8e502e-1000/20220621_circular_2022_40_en.zip |title=Invitation to bid for the 2023/24 and 2024/25 UEFA Europa Conference League finals and the 2024/25 UEFA Women's Champions League final|magazine=UEFA Circular Letter |publisher=Union of European Football Associations|number=40/2022 |date=21 June 2022 |access-date=28 June 2022}}</ref>

*21 June 2022: Applications formally invited
*31 August 2022: Closing date for registering intention to bid
*7 September 2022: Bid requirements made available to bidders
*3 November 2022: Submission of preliminary bid dossier
*23 February 2023: Submission of final bid dossier
*28 June 2023: Appointment of host

The UEFA Executive Committee appointed the Agia Sophia Stadium as the host during their meeting in [[Nyon]], Switzerland, on 28 June 2023. As the stadium was new and had never been operated by the club for international competitions, it was subject to an observation period until November 2023 for matches played in UEFA club competitions and [[UEFA Euro 2024 qualifying]]. A date of December 2023 was set for official confirmation that the venue met the requirements.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/0282-185d513bd18e-32f59d9c7dea-1000/ |title=Lisbon to host UEFA Women's Champions League final in 2025 |work=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=28 June 2023 |access-date=28 June 2023}}</ref>

